ity is roughly 0.03-0.06, far short of the 0.6 threshold. Event volume is also flat across bins (147-222), ruling out a burst-driven artifact, and high total activity / node popularity alone (step_29) does not indicate a switch. The internal turnover is in *who* is speaking, not in *whom they speak to* - there is no old-contacts-to-new-contacts substitution. Decision: absent."}, "raw_final_response": "The evidence is clear and consistent across all sub-windows.\n\n**Analysis:** The group's 12 members are active in a *staggered relay* \u2014 each member fires in a different 5000-unit sub-window (214/315/522 early, 860/919 next, 1085, then 1390/1431, then 1589/1672, then 1860/1936 last). But every one of them contacts the *same* fixed set of ~30 partners `{123, 160, 208, 283, 350, 462, 616, 829, 861, 871, 899, 945, 1057, 1064, 1088, 1097, 1194, 1277, 1377, 1442, 1449, 1519, 1574, 1610, 1660, 1670, 1884, 1934, 1947, 1950}`. So the *group-level* contact set is invariant over time: at any candidate change point, before-set \u2248 after-set, Jaccard \u2248 0.94\u20131.0, dissimilarity \u2248 0.03\u20130.06 \u2014 far below the 0.6 threshold. Volume is also flat (147\u2013222 events per bin), so there is no burst masquerading as a switch.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_8b4a3bfa\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [214, 315, 522, 860, 919, 1085, 1390, 1431, 1589, 1672, 1860, 1936],\n  \"window\": [2551, 32551],\n  \"confidence\": 0.9,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_16\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_17\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_18\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_19\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_20\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_21\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_22\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_23\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_24\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_25\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_26\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_27\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_28\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_10\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_13\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_14\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_15\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_29\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_31\", \"role\": \"support\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"The group shows a staggered activity relay: individual members are active in disjoint 5000-unit sub-windows (214/315/522 in [2551,7551]; 522/860/919 in [7551,12551]; 919/1085 in [12551,17551]; 1390/1431 in [17551,22551]; 1431/1589/1672 in [22551,27551]; 1672/1860/1936 in [27551,32551]). However, every active member contacts the identical fixed roster of ~30 partners (123,160,208,...,1950). Consequently the group's aggregated before-set and after-set at every candidate change point are nearly identical: Jaccard 0.94-1.0, so peak dissimilarity is roughly 0.03-0.06, far short of the 0.6 threshold. Event volume is also flat across bins (147-222), ruling out a burst-driven artifact, and high total activity / node popularity alone (step_29) does not indicate a switch. The internal turnover is in *who* is speaking, not in *whom they speak to* - there is no old-contacts-to-new-contacts substitution. Decision: absent.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 70476, "total_output_tokens": 8089, "to